---
layout: base.njk
permalink: /charts/index.html
title: Charts
---

<header class="detail-head">
	<h2 class="page-hed">Charts</h2>
	<p class="lede">
		The corpus seen all at once rather than one site at a time. Every chart
		here is drawn from the most recent measurement of every site, so it
		describes where things stand today &mdash; not how any one site got there,
		which is what its own page is for.
	</p>
</header>

{% from "scatter.njk" import scatterfigure %}
{% from "strip.njk" import stripfigure %}

{#
	Built rather than written out, so a chart that has no data to draw drops out
	of the list instead of leaving a link to a section that is not on the page.
#}
{% set toc = [] %}
{% if sites.lcpScatter %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "weight-lcp", label: "Page Weight" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% if sites.jsScatter %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "js-lcp", label: "JavaScript Weight" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% if sites.ttfbScatter %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "ttfb-lcp", label: "Time to First Byte" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% if sites.fontClsScatter %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "font-cls", label: "Fonts and Layout Shift" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% if sites.thirdPartyScatter %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "third-party", label: "Third Party" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% if sites.jsByGenerator %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "js-by-generator", label: "JavaScript by Generator" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% if sites.ttfbByHost %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "ttfb-by-host", label: "TTFB by Host" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% if sites.labFieldPairs %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "lab-field", label: "Lab vs Field LCP" }), toc) %}{% endif %}

{% if toc.length > 1 %}
<nav class="contents contents-sticky" aria-label="On this page">
	<span class="contents-label">On this page</span>
	<ul>
		{% for item in toc %}
		<li><a href="#{{ item.id }}">{{ item.label }}</a></li>
		{% endfor %}
	</ul>
</nav>
{% endif %}

{% if sites.lcpScatter %}
{% set plot = sites.lcpScatter %}
<section class="group" id="weight-lcp">
	<div class="group-head">
		<h2>Page Weight vs LCP <span class="tag">{{ plot.count | num }} sites</span></h2>
	</div>
	<p class="muted small">
		Every site's most recent measurement: how much the page weighed against how
		long its largest element took to paint. Both scales are logarithmic &mdash;
		the corpus runs from a few hundred bytes to 53&nbsp;MB, and no linear axis
		can show that and the detail at the same time.
	</p>
	{#
		Said rather than left to be inferred. A reader who hovers a red dot and
		gets nothing will assume the page is broken; told first, the silence reads
		as deliberate — which it is. These are other people's sites, and a chart
		about a relationship should not double as a list of who is worst.
	#}
	<p class="muted small">
		Only the {{ plot.labelled | num }} dots that painted within 2.5&nbsp;s are
		named &mdash; hover one for the site and its figures, or follow it through
		to that site's page. The slower dots are plotted but deliberately left
		unlabelled. Without a mouse, {{ plot.keyboardCount }} of them spread across
		the weight axis are in the tab order.
	</p>
	{{ scatterfigure(plot, "Page Weight") }}
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if sites.jsScatter %}
{% set js = sites.jsScatter %}
<section class="group" id="js-lcp">
	<div class="group-head">
		<h2>JavaScript Weight vs LCP <span class="tag">{{ js.count | num }} sites</span></h2>
	</div>
	<p class="muted small">
		The same picture drawn against scripts alone rather than the whole page.
		Total weight is mostly images, which a site can defer or resize; script
		weight is the part it chose to run before anything could paint.
	</p>
	{#
		The zero column is the finding, so it is named rather than left as an
		oddity in the corner of the axis. A fifth of the corpus ships no script at
		all, and a log scale has no coordinate for that — without a column of
		their own those sites would simply be missing, and the chart would show a
		relationship with its clearest counter-example deleted.
	#}
	{% if js.zero %}
	<p class="muted small">
		The column left of the break is the {{ js.zero.count | num }}
		{{ js.zero.count | plural("site") }} that ship no JavaScript at all. Zero
		has no place on a logarithmic scale &mdash; it is not a small number there
		but an absent one &mdash; so they are set apart rather than dropped or
		flattened in against the smallest measured value.
	</p>
	{% endif %}
	<p class="muted small">
		Naming works as above: the {{ js.labelled | num }} dots inside 2.5&nbsp;s
		are named on hover, {{ js.keyboardCount }} of them in the tab order.
	</p>
	{{ scatterfigure(js, "JavaScript Weight") }}
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if sites.ttfbScatter %}
{% set ttfb = sites.ttfbScatter %}
<section class="group" id="ttfb-lcp">
	<div class="group-head">
		<h2>TTFB vs LCP <span class="tag">{{ ttfb.count | num }} sites</span></h2>
	</div>
	<p class="muted small">
		Time to First Byte along the bottom, largest contentful paint up the side
		&mdash; the wait before the browser had anything at all, against the wait
		until the page looked loaded. This is the part of LCP a site cannot fix by
		shipping less: nothing renders until the server replies.
	</p>
	{#
		An earlier draft said every dot must sit above the line where the two are
		equal, since nothing can paint before the first byte. Four sites say
		otherwise — seccl.tech reports a 27.7 s first byte against a 3.1 s paint —
		because the two figures come from different runs of the same measurement,
		and the median of each is taken separately. A tempting claim, and checking
		it was cheaper than being wrong in public.

		Both axes are milliseconds here, unlike the two charts above, so the
		numbers on them are directly comparable.
	#}
	<p class="muted small">
		Naming works as above: the {{ ttfb.labelled | num }} dots inside 2.5&nbsp;s
		are named on hover, {{ ttfb.keyboardCount }} of them in the tab order.
	</p>
	{{ scatterfigure(ttfb, "Time to First Byte") }}
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if sites.fontClsScatter %}
{% set fonts = sites.fontClsScatter %}
<section class="group" id="font-cls">
	<div class="group-head">
		<h2>Web Font Weight vs CLS <span class="tag">{{ fonts.count | num }} sites</span></h2>
	</div>
	<p class="muted small">
		Font bytes along the bottom, cumulative layout shift up the side &mdash; the
		question being whether the text arriving late drags the page around under
		the reader. Colours and the two lines are the CLS thresholds, 0.1 and 0.25,
		not the paint-time ones used above.
	</p>
	{#
		Both zero bands are stated plainly. Between them they cover most of the
		corpus, and a reader who takes the log scales at face value would badly
		misjudge how much of the picture is "none of this happens at all".
	#}
	<p class="muted small">
		{% if fonts.zero %}The column left of the vertical break is the
		{{ fonts.zero.count | num }} {{ fonts.zero.count | plural("site") }} loading
		no font file.{% endif %}
		{% if fonts.zeroY %}The row below the horizontal one is the
		{{ fonts.zeroY.count | num }} that never shift at all &mdash; more than half
		of everything measured.{% endif %}
		Neither zero has a place on a logarithmic scale, so both are set apart
		rather than dropped.
	</p>
	{{ scatterfigure(fonts, "Web Font Weight", "Cumulative Layout Shift") }}
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if sites.thirdPartyScatter %}
{% set tp = sites.thirdPartyScatter %}
<section class="group" id="third-party">
	<div class="group-head">
		<h2>Third Party vs Performance <span class="tag">{{ tp.count | num }} sites</span></h2>
	</div>
	<p class="muted small">
		Bytes served from somebody else's domain, against the Lighthouse
		performance score &mdash; analytics, embeds, fonts, tag managers, ads.
		Every other chart here plots a measurement against a measurement; this one
		ends in a verdict, which is why it reads the other way round.
	</p>
	{#
		Two things differ from the charts above, and both would mislead if left
		unsaid. The vertical scale is linear because a score is bounded — zero has
		no place on a log axis and the step from 90 to 100 is not smaller than the
		one from 10 to 20. And the colours are inverted, because on this axis a
		high number is the good one.
	#}
	<p class="muted small">
		The score runs 0 to 100 on a straight scale, and green is at the top:
		<strong>{{ tp.counts.good | num }}</strong> sites score 90 or better,
		{{ tp.counts.average | num }} land between 50 and 90, and
		{{ tp.counts.poor | num }} fall below 50.
		{% if tp.zero %}The column left of the break is the
		{{ tp.zero.count | num }} {{ tp.zero.count | plural("site") }} that load
		nothing from anyone else at all.{% endif %}
	</p>
	<p class="muted small">
		Naming works as above: the {{ tp.labelled | num }} sites scoring 90 or
		better are named on hover, {{ tp.keyboardCount }} of them in the tab order.
	</p>
	{{ scatterfigure(tp, "Third-Party Weight", "Performance Score") }}
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if sites.jsByGenerator %}
{% set jg = sites.jsByGenerator %}
<section class="group" id="js-by-generator">
	<div class="group-head">
		<h2>JavaScript Weight by Site Generator <span class="tag">{{ jg.count | num }} sites</span></h2>
	</div>
	<p class="muted small">
		One row per generator, one dot per site, ordered by median. The dot behind
		the label is that generator's median; the spread either side of it is the
		part a single figure cannot show.
	</p>
	{#
		The caveat is not a footnote. Eleventy is four fifths of the sites that
		report a generator here, and every other row is a handful of sites that
		someone chose to submit to an Eleventy project. Read down the column of
		counts before reading across the rows.
	#}
	<p class="muted small">
		Across {{ jg.groups }} generators with at least five sites each &mdash;
		twenty more were detected too rarely to say anything about.
		{% if jg.zero %}The column left of the break is the
		{{ jg.zero.count | num }} sites shipping no script at all.{% endif %}
		Build Awesome and Eleventy are counted as one row, being one project under
		two names. Sample sizes are on every row, and they are lopsided: this is an
		Eleventy project's corpus, so it describes these sites rather than the web.
	</p>

	{{ stripfigure(jg, "JavaScript Weight") }}
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if sites.ttfbByHost %}
{% set th = sites.ttfbByHost %}
<section class="group" id="ttfb-by-host">
	<div class="group-head">
		<h2>TTFB by Host <span class="tag">{{ th.count | num }} sites</span></h2>
	</div>
	<p class="muted small">
		The same strip plot as above, over where a site is served from rather than
		what built it. Time to first byte is the one figure on this page a host is
		directly responsible for &mdash; everything else is a consequence of what
		the page then does.
	</p>
	{#
		The negative result is the point, so it is stated rather than left to be
		inferred from rows that look alike. The generator chart's medians span
		eighty-fold; these span one and a half, and the rows overlap almost
		entirely. Choosing a host moves this number far less than the variation
		within any one of them.
	#}
	<p class="muted small">
		Across {{ th.groups }} hosts with at least five sites each. The medians run
		from {{ th.rows[0].medianText }} to {{ th.rows[th.rows.length - 1].medianText }}
		&mdash; a spread of {{ th.medianSpread }}&times;, against
		{{ sites.jsByGenerator.medianSpread }}&times; between generators on the
		chart above. The rows overlap almost entirely, which is worth reading as
		the finding rather than as a flat picture: on this evidence, which host you
		pick moves your first byte very little next to how much any one host varies.
	</p>
	{{ stripfigure(th, "Time to First Byte") }}
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if sites.labFieldPairs %}
{% set lf = sites.labFieldPairs %}
<section class="group" id="lab-field">
	<div class="group-head">
		<h2>Lab vs Field LCP <span class="tag">{{ lf.count | num }} sites</span></h2>
	</div>
	<p class="muted small">
		One row per site: a dot for what our throttled lab run measured, a dot for
		what real users actually got &mdash; Chrome&rsquo;s field p75 &mdash; and a
		line joining them. The line is the disagreement, and its length is that
		site&rsquo;s error in seconds. Rows are ordered by the field figure, so the
		coloured dots form a curve and the lab dots scatter against it.
	</p>
	{#
		The sampling bias is the first thing to say, not a footnote. CrUX only
		reports sites with enough traffic to anonymise, so this is not a seventh of
		the corpus chosen at random — it is the popular seventh, which is likely
		the better-resourced one too. Any reading of the aggregate carries that.
	#}
	<p class="muted small">
		The lab is slower than reality on <strong>{{ lf.labSlower | num }}</strong>
		of these and faster on {{ lf.labFaster | num }}. Hovering names the
		{{ lf.labelled | num }} whose real-user paint came inside 2.5&nbsp;s; the
		slower rows are drawn but not identified. Only {{ lf.count | num }} sites
		appear at all, because Chrome publishes field data only for sites with
		enough traffic to anonymise &mdash; not a random sample of the
		{{ sites.stats.measured | num }} measured here but the best-trafficked part
		of it, so read the spread rather than the average.
	</p>

	<figure class="plot">
		<div class="plot-frame">
		<svg viewBox="0 0 {{ lf.box.w }} {{ lf.box.h }}" role="img"
			aria-label="Lab and field largest contentful paint for {{ lf.count | num }} sites, each pair joined by a line. The lab figure is slower than the field figure on {{ lf.labSlower | num }} of them.">
			<g class="plot-grid">
				{%- for tick in lf.xTicks %}
				<line x1="{{ tick.x }}" x2="{{ tick.x }}" y1="{{ lf.box.top }}" y2="{{ lf.box.top + lf.plot.h }}"/>
				{%- endfor %}
			</g>

			<g class="plot-threshold">
				{%- for line in lf.thresholds %}
				<line x1="{{ line.x }}" x2="{{ line.x }}" y1="{{ lf.box.top }}" y2="{{ lf.box.top + lf.plot.h }}"/>
				<text x="{{ line.x + 5 }}" y="{{ lf.box.top + 12 }}" text-anchor="start">{{ line.label }}</text>
				{%- endfor %}
			</g>

			<g class="plot-axis">
				{%- for tick in lf.xTicks %}
				<text x="{{ tick.x }}" y="{{ lf.axis.tickY }}" text-anchor="middle">{{ tick.label }}</text>
				{%- endfor %}
			</g>
			<g class="plot-axis-title">
				<text x="{{ lf.axis.xLabel.x }}" y="{{ lf.axis.xLabel.y }}" text-anchor="middle">Largest Contentful Paint</text>
			</g>

			{#
				Lines first, then the lab dots, then the field dots on top — the
				field end is the one that carries the verdict, so nothing is allowed
				to sit over it.
			#}
			<g class="pair-links">
				{%- for pair in lf.pairs %}<line x1="{{ pair.labX }}" x2="{{ pair.fieldX }}" y1="{{ pair.y }}" y2="{{ pair.y }}"/>{% endfor %}
			</g>
			<g class="pair-lab">
				{%- for pair in lf.pairs %}<circle cx="{{ pair.labX }}" cy="{{ pair.y }}" r="2"/>{% endfor %}
			</g>
			{%- for band in ["poor", "average", "good"] %}
			<g class="pair-field plot-{{ band }}">
				{%- for pair in lf.pairs %}{% if pair.band == band %}<circle cx="{{ pair.fieldX }}" cy="{{ pair.y }}" r="2.2"/>{% endif %}{% endfor %}
			</g>
			{%- endfor %}
		</svg>

		{#
			One hit target per row rather than per dot: the pair is the unit being
			read, and a 4px row is already the smallest thing here worth aiming at.

			Only the rows real users found fast are named, as on every chart here.
			The others are still drawn, and still every bit as legible — they are
			simply not identified.
		#}
		<div class="plot-hits">
			{%- for pair in lf.pairs %}{% if pair.named %}
			<a class="plot-hit pair-hit{{ ' is-low' if pair.flipY }}"
				href="/site/{{ pair.slug }}/" style="top: {{ pair.py }}%; height: {{ lf.rowPct }}%"
				tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
				<span class="plot-tip">
					<span class="plot-tip-name">{{ pair.name }}</span>
					<span class="plot-tip-stats">lab {{ pair.labText }} · field {{ pair.fieldText }}</span>
				</span>
			</a>
			{%- endif %}{% endfor %}
		</div>
		</div>

		<figcaption class="plot-legend">
			<span class="plot-key pair-key-lab">Lab</span>
			<span class="plot-key plot-good">Field, within 2.5&nbsp;s</span>
			<span class="plot-key plot-average">Field, to 4&nbsp;s</span>
			<span class="plot-key plot-poor">Field, over 4&nbsp;s</span>
		</figcaption>
	</figure>
</section>
{% endif %}
